From 419712dac9f455fb9ca3b0dfb0c655d03895fc4e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Romeo Mattar <romeomat.work@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 12:09:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] hw/misc: add sim-gpio, host-driven GPIO lines on the virtual
 clock

Firmware tests need to press buttons and watch LEDs at exact points in
the guest's timeline.  Driving a GPIO input from QMP or the monitor
happens at a host-dependent moment, so two runs of the same test differ.

sim-gpio is a bus-less device with N GPIO outputs a host drives and N
GPIO inputs it watches, over a line-based chardev protocol.  Level
changes carry a QEMU_CLOCK_VIRTUAL timestamp and are applied by a
virtual-clock timer, the same scheme as i2c-sim-sensor; every change of
a watched input is reported with its virtual time.  The device never
waits for the host to drive a line.  Reports use a blocking write, since
they are the record of what the guest did.

Boards decide which pads the lines are wired to.

Signed-off-by: Romeo Mattar <romeomat.work@gmail.com>
---
 hw/misc/Kconfig            |   4 +
 hw/misc/meson.build        |   1 +
 hw/misc/sim_gpio.c         | 284 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 include/hw/misc/sim_gpio.h |  17 +++
 4 files changed, 306 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 hw/misc/sim_gpio.c
 create mode 100644 include/hw/misc/sim_gpio.h

diff --git a/hw/misc/Kconfig b/hw/misc/Kconfig
index 7836bd4..6869b12 100644
--- a/hw/misc/Kconfig
+++ b/hw/misc/Kconfig
@@ -220,3 +220,7 @@ source macio/Kconfig
 config SIM_CLOCK
     bool
     default y
+
+config SIM_GPIO
